In the discussion, James outlined a plan to create a JavaScript-orchestrated `Planner` for AI responses, emphasizing a synchronous chat-first abstraction that selects execution plans and allows adaptive tool usage. The conversation progressed through various troubleshooting issues, including model behavior regarding optional execution plans and a `KeyError` related to missing tool names in JavaScript execution context. The solution involved tightening the prompts to disallow secondary planning during response synthesis and ensuring clearer instructions for tool use. Emphasis was placed on developing a protocol that differentiates between planning, synthesis, and user-triggered continuation to enhance clarity and control in AI interactions. The need for robust error handling and user inputs was also discussed to optimize system performance.
